#
# Original header, for ec/tools/check_testdata_row_claims.py alone:
# ec/tools/check_testdata_row_claims.py, wired into the cheap gate. Prepared
# here rather than landed, for the reason every other file in docs/ci/ is:
# `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is copied from the agent-pipeline template
# and the pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it
# fails at the end of the PR rather than at the start of it.
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this across a template re-copy as its item
# 10, beside the item 9 that says the same for the index checker.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-testdata-row-claims.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# It is a patch of its own, and it still composes with
# `agent-gates-capture-claims.patch`, which issue #745 had to fold two
# patches into one. The two inserts into the seven-line `gate` list are the
# part that cannot be re-anchored, so this one goes after `gate 'doc links'`
# and lands last, where #745's patch's context ends at `gate 'shellcheck'`
# and the two windows no longer touch. The function goes above
# `check_register_counts()` rather than beside it, which is the other half:
# #745's patch anchors there, and two inserts at one anchor are the case that
# started this. `tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py` applies the set in every
# ordered pair and asserts the result still parses, so no landing order has to
# be written down anywhere.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not. The index over `ec/tools/testdata/` is
# written by hand, and `check_testdata_index.py` already holds it to the tree
# in four directions -- but it reads the first column, the `Feeds` column and
# the nested tables, and not the third. The third is the description: the
# column a reader opens the index to read, and the one that names the
# addresses each fixture is supposed to contain. The two hand-repairs
# measured over their pre-repair trees (#502, #720) were to that column, and
# every check on the index was green through both; the other two of the four
# the history holds (#1008) are in the same column. **Not claimed: that this
# would have caught any of them** -- what those repairs changed is in the
# issues, not in the tree, and the PR says so in its own words.
#
# The rule is an address a row's third column attributes to a fixture having
# to occur in a file that row names, searched across the whole set the row's
# first column resolves to rather than per file. The closed shape list, whose
# entries the tool writes down as `SHAPES`, are counted, printed with the
# reason, and do not fail the run -- a checker that reported its own blind spot
# as a broken index would be pushed to grow a rule for whatever it could not
# read, and would end up inventing the thing it is checking. Two of those
# entries are dated refusals, a capture named in prose that resolves to nothing
# and a sentence naming two of them. The list is closed and each entry has a
# case.
#
# **Until a human lands this, no commit runs it.** The suite
# (`ec/tools/test_check_testdata_row_claims.py`) needs no wiring to be run at
# all: `tools/run-tests.sh` discovers every `test_*.py` in the repository, so
# it is already collected by the runner.
#
# The comment the function adds at the call site says the same thing where the
# next reader of the gate will see it, rather than only here.
