+# Not a style check either: the index over ec/tools/testdata/ is hand-written,
+# and the column a reader checks it by -- the third, the description -- names
+# the addresses each fixture is supposed to carry. Nothing read that column, so
+# a row could say a fixture differs at 0x0784 and the fixture need not, and the
+# index has needed a hand-repair four times (#182, #502, #720, #736), each in a
+# description of a row whose file was already named correctly. This holds those
+# sentences to the files the row names, across the whole set its first column
+# resolves to rather than per file: 0x075B is in one of
+# 0751-isolation-run-staged/'s three CSVs and in neither of the other two,
+# while the row names the directory. Cheap tier because of what it reads -- the
+# committed index, the committed tree under ec/tools/testdata/ and two
+# annotation CSVs, no firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no assembler. It
+# prints what it checked and how many literals it could not place, because a
+# run that checked nothing and a run that found nothing look the same from the
+# exit code alone, and because "unresolved" there means not checked by this
+# method and never absent. Not claimed: that it would have caught #502 or
+# #720 -- what those repairs changed is in the issues, not in the tree.
+check_testdata_row_claims() {
+  python3 ec/tools/check_testdata_row_claims.py --check
+}
