 }
 
+# The third column of ec/tools/testdata/README.md is the one a reader opens the
+# index to read, and it makes two kinds of claim about each fixture: which
+# addresses the fixture carries, and what grade_0751_isolation.py prints over
+# it. check_testdata_index.py and check_testdata_row_claims.py hold the first to
+# the tree; this holds the second, by running the grader over the set each row's
+# first column resolves to and comparing what it printed to what the row says of
+# it. That is the gap issue #978 measured and named -- both checkers reported 0
+# missing over the pre-repair tree at each of the index's hand-repairs, because
+# every row those repairs touched spells zero backticked addresses -- and
+# docs/findings/testdata-grader-claims.md is what closes it. Cheap tier because
+# of what it reads: the committed index, the committed tree under
+# ec/tools/testdata/, and the grader's own output over it -- no firmware image,
+# no Ghidra, no network. It prints a census of which rows carry a claim and
+# which do not, because the answer is a mix and a reader who cannot tell an
+# unchecked row from a clean one cannot tell whether the run covered anything,
+# and "unresolved" there means not checked by this method and never absent. Not
+# claimed: that a fixture demonstrates what its row says about the EC. This
+# compares the index's sentence to the grader's output, and the grader's output
+# is itself a claim about a capture no live run has produced.
+check_testdata_grader_claims() {
+  python3 ec/tools/check_testdata_grader_claims.py --check
+}
+
+# A figure written down is not a pin. The census re-derivation checklist's 2b
+# told a re-deriver which of its numbers to redo by hand and was wrong in both
+# directions: it sent readers to redo work the tree already does, and left the
+# numbers nothing held looking like company. This resolves every figure in a
+# named section's verdict tables to one of four verdicts -- a module-level
+# constant whose key is read outside its own span, an int inside a check() or
+# numeric assert* call, unheld, or not read by this method -- and prints the
+# file:line that decided it. A held row's pin has to resolve three ways: the
+# file exists, the line is one it has, and it is the file the measurement
+# resolved to, so a pin that has drifted onto the wrong module is reported
+# rather than counted. Cheap tier because of what it reads: committed markdown
+# and committed python, no firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no assembler.
+# Every unheld is "not found by this method", never "absent", and the four
+# limits are in the tool's docstring rather than here. Not claimed: that it
+# would have caught a figure that decayed -- it reports what a search over
+# committed text finds, so a pin it cannot see is one this method did not find.
+check_doc_figure_pins() {
+  python3 ec/tools/check_doc_figure_pins.py \
+    docs/findings/xdata-census-rederivation-checklist.md --section 2b
+}
